window.docContents[153].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-simple-graphs-on":{"contents":"If G is a graph with vertex type ℕ and decidable adjacency, then we get\nLocallyLTFinite for free, since G.neighborLTSet v is a subset of the finite set\n{0, 1, …, v - 1}: any vertex v : ℕ has at most v earlier neighbours! So for\nnow we will restrict our attention to such graphs, and to keep things simple we will\nalso use ℕ as our set of colours. (Later we will show how to easily extend our\ngreedy colourings to any constructively countable vertex type.)\n\nAny SimpleGraph ℕ with decidable adjacency is LocallyLTFinite.\n\nsection greedy\nvariable (G : SimpleGraph ℕ) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n\ninstance : G.LocallyLTFinite :=\n  fun v => Fintype.ofFinset {u < v | G.Adj v u} (by simp)\n\n\nGiven an assignment c of colours to {0, 1, ..., v - 1}, we want to define the\ncolour of v as the smallest natural number that is not used by any of its earlier\nneighbours. Rather than actually taking c to be a function defined only on\n{0, 1, ..., v - 1}, we will instead take c : ℕ → ℕ but then ignore the values of\nc at vertices u ≥ v.\n\nSo given c : ℕ → ℕ and a vertex v : ℕ, how should we define the greedy choice of\ncolour at v? The most direct route uses Nat.find: given a decidable predicate\np : ℕ → Prop and a proof that there exists an n such that p n, Nat.find\nreturns the least such n.\n\n#check Nat.find      -- Nat.find {p : ℕ → Prop} [DecidablePred p] (H : ∃ n, p n) : ℕ\n#check Nat.find_spec -- .. (H : ∃ n, p n) : p (Nat.find H)\n#check Nat.find_min  -- .. (H : ∃ n, p n) {m : ℕ} : m < Nat.find H → ¬p m\n#check Nat.find_min' -- .. (H : ∃ n, p n) {m : ℕ} (h : p m) : Nat.find H ≤ m\n\n\nTo use this for the choice of colour at vertex v, we need a proof that at least one\nnatural number is not used by any of the earlier neighbours of v. The colours they\nuse form the finite set (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c, and since ℕ is infinite,\nMathlib's Finset.exists_notMem tells us that some natural number lies outside it.\n\n#check Finset.exists_notMem -- .. [Infinite α] (s : Finset α) : ∃ a, a ∉ s\n\n\nIn this definition we give the set of used colours a name with let. Without this,\nthe set of used colours sits inside the predicate that Nat.find tests, and is\nrecomputed for every candidate colour.\n\nGiven any c : ℕ → ℕ and a vertex v : ℕ, G.greedyChoice c v is the least\ncolour that is valid at v (in the sense that no earlier neighbour of v uses this\ncolour)./-- Given any c : ℕ → ℕ and a vertex v : ℕ, G.greedyChoice c v is the least\ncolour that is valid at v (in the sense that no earlier neighbour of v uses this\ncolour). -/\ndef greedyChoice (c : ℕ → ℕ) (v : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  let used := (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c\n  Nat.find used.exists_notMem\n\n\nThe colour chosen at vertex v is at most the number of distinct colours used by\nits earlier neighbours.\n\nlemma greedyChoice_le_card_image (c : ℕ → ℕ) (v : ℕ) :\n    G.greedyChoice c v ≤ #((G.neighborLTFinset v).image c) := by\n  have hsub : range (G.greedyChoice c v) ⊆ (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c := by\n    intro u hu\n    exact not_not.mp <| Nat.find_min (Finset.exists_notMem _) (mem_range.mp hu)\n  simpa using card_le_card hsub\n\n\nThe colour chosen at vertex v is never used by any earlier neighbour.\n\nlemma greedyChoice_ne_of_mem_neighborLTFinset (c : ℕ → ℕ) {v u : ℕ}\n    (hu : u ∈ G.neighborLTFinset v) : G.greedyChoice c v ≠ c u := by\n  intro hf\n  exact Nat.find_spec (Finset.exists_notMem _) (mem_image.mpr ⟨u, hu, hf.symm⟩)\n\n\nFinally, greedyChoice c n only inspects the partial colouring c on the earlier\nneighbours of n, so two partial colourings that agree below n make the same\ngreedy choice at n.\n\ngreedyChoice c n only depends on the values of c below n./-- greedyChoice c n only depends on the values of c below n. -/\nlemma greedyChoice_congr {c₁ c₂ : ℕ → ℕ} {n : ℕ} (h : ∀ m < n, c₁ m = c₂ m) :\n    G.greedyChoice c₁ n = G.greedyChoice c₂ n := by\n  simp_all [greedyChoice]\n\n\nLeft: a greedy colouring, with colours 0, 1, 2 and 3 drawn as red, blue, green and yellow. Right: for each vertex n, its earlier neighbours, the colours they forbid, and the least colour not forbidden\n\nWe use greedyChoice to define the greedy colouring greedy of G by\nwell-founded recursion on ℕ. To colour a vertex v we need to know the colours\nof its earlier vertices u < v, so the value of greedy at v is defined in\nterms of its values at strictly smaller arguments.\n\nConcretely, we feed greedyChoice the \"partial colouring\" function\nfun u ↦ if u < v then greedy u else 42. The if u < v guard makes the recursion\nwell-founded. The recursive call greedy u occurs only in the then branch, where\nu < v holds, and this is what Lean's termination checker needs to prove that the\ncall is at a strictly smaller argument. The 42 in the else branch is just filler;\nits value is irrelevant, since greedyChoice ignores the values of the partial\ncolouring at u ≥ v.\n\nThe greedy ℕ-colouring of G as a function, defined using well-founded\nrecursion on ℕ./-- The greedy ℕ-colouring of G as a function, defined using well-founded\nrecursion on ℕ. -/\ndef greedy (v : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  G.greedyChoice (fun u ↦ if u < v then greedy u else 42) v\n\n\nNotice that we didn't need to prove that greedy is well-defined: by working over\nℕ Lean can automatically discharge the termination proof for us. (Under the hood\nthis is using WellFounded.Nat.fix.)\n\n#print greedy -- .. WellFounded.Nat.fix ..\n\n\nWe can unfold greedy with rw [greedy]. Since the partial colouring used at v\nagrees with greedy below v, greedyChoice_congr lets us drop the if\naltogether. Viewing G.greedyChoice as a map from colourings ℕ → ℕ to colourings\nℕ → ℕ, this says that greedy is a fixed point of it:\ngreedy = G.greedyChoice greedy.\n\nlemma greedy_eq_greedyChoice (v : ℕ) : G.greedy v = G.greedyChoice G.greedy v := by\n  rw [greedy]\n  exact G.greedyChoice_congr fun u hu => ite_eq_left hu\n\n\nThe greedy colour of v is at most the number of distinct colours used by\ngreedy on the earlier neighbours of v.\n\nlemma greedy_le_card_image (v : ℕ) :\n    G.greedy v ≤ #((G.neighborLTFinset v).image G.greedy) :=\n  G.greedy_eq_greedyChoice v ▸ G.greedyChoice_le_card_image _ v\n\n\nIn particular the greedy colour of v is at most the number of earlier neighbours\nof v, and hence is less than degreeLT v + 1.\n\nlemma greedy_lt (v : ℕ) : G.greedy v < G.degreeLT v + 1 :=\n  Nat.lt_succ_iff.2 <| (G.greedy_le_card_image v).trans card_image_le\n\n\nIf u < v and v is adjacent to u then the greedy colour of v is not equal to\nthe greedy colour of u. (The line variable {G} in makes G an implicit\nargument of this lemma only, since it can be read off from hadj.)\n\nvariable {G} in\nlemma Adj.greedy_ne_of_gt {u v : ℕ} (hadj : G.Adj v u) (h : u < v) :\n    G.greedy v ≠ G.greedy u :=\n  G.greedy_eq_greedyChoice v ▸\n    G.greedyChoice_ne_of_mem_neighborLTFinset _ (by simp_all)\n\n\nSo any pair of adjacent vertices u and v in G receive different colours in the\ngreedy colouring.\n\nvariable {G} in\nlemma Adj.greedy_ne {u v : ℕ} (hadj : G.Adj u v) :\n    G.greedy u ≠ G.greedy v := by\n  rcases hadj.ne.lt_or_gt with h | h\n  · exact (hadj.symm.greedy_ne_of_gt h).symm\n  · exact hadj.greedy_ne_of_gt h\n\n\nTogether these results pin down the greedy colour exactly when the earlier neighbours\nof v use precisely the colours 0, 1, ..., k - 1: the colour of v is then k.\n(This very special lemma will be useful when we look at the crown graph later.)\n\nlemma greedy_eq_of_image_eq_range {v k : ℕ}\n    (h : (G.neighborLTFinset v).image G.greedy = range k) : G.greedy v = k := by\n  refine le_antisymm (by simpa [h] using G.greedy_le_card_image v) ?_\n  by_contra! hlt\n  obtain ⟨m, hm, hgm⟩ := mem_image.mp (h ▸ mem_range.2 hlt)\n  exact ((G.mem_neighborLTFinset v m).1 hm).2.greedy_ne hgm.symm\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*","header":"7.8.2. Simple Graphs on  ℕ","id":"/Colorings/#section-simple-graphs-on"},"/Theorems/#section-k-nig-s-lemma":{"contents":"Kőnig's infinity lemma D. Kőnig, Über eine Schlußweise aus dem Endlichen ins Unendliche [On a mode of inference from the finite to the infinite], Acta Litt. Sci. Szeged 3 (1927), 121–130. (not to be confused with Kőnig's theorem\non matchings) says that an infinite, connected, locally finite graph contains a ray\n(a one-way infinite path). Mathlib has the order-theoretic form of this result: in a\n(strongly atomic) partial order in which each element is covered by finitely many\nelements, an element with infinitely many elements above it starts an infinite chain\nb = f 0 ⋖ f 1 ⋖ f 2 ⋖ ⋯ of covering steps.\n\n#check exists_seq_covby_of_forall_covby_finite\n\n\nMathlib has no notion of a ray in a graph, so it does not yet state the graph form.\nA ray in G is a copy of the infinite path pathNat, so the graph form says that\npathNat ⊑ G for every infinite, connected, locally finite graph G. We deduce it\nfrom the order-theoretic form.\n\nFix a vertex v and order the vertices so that u ≤ w iff u lies on a shortest\npath from v to w, that is, iff G.dist v u + G.dist u w = G.dist v w. We use\ndist rather than edist: as G is connected, dist has no junk values, and the\nproof cancels terms from sums, which does not work in ℕ∞. Inside the proof,\nlet _ : PartialOrder V makes this order a local instance, so the instance search\nfinds it for the rest of the proof and nowhere else.\n\nWe then check the hypotheses of Kőnig's lemma. The function G.dist v is strictly\nmonotone, so the order is well founded, and Mathlib has an instance saying that a\nwell-founded order is strongly atomic: if u < w then some x ≤ w covers u. If\nw covers u then u and w are adjacent: the second vertex x of a shortest\nwalk from u to w satisfies u ≤ x ≤ w and x ≠ u, so x = w. So the vertices\ncovering u are neighbours of u, and there are finitely many of them. Finally,\nevery vertex lies above v, so infinitely many vertices do.\n\nKőnig's lemma now gives a chain v = f 0 ⋖ f 1 ⋖ f 2 ⋖ ⋯ whose consecutive vertices\nare adjacent. We use the version\nexists_orderEmbedding_covby_of_forall_covby_finite, which returns the chain as an\norder embedding f : ℕ ↪o V, so the injectivity needed for a copy of pathNat comes\nfor free.\n\nKőnig's lemma: an infinite, connected, locally finite graph has a ray./-- **Kőnig's lemma**: an infinite, connected, locally finite graph has a ray. -/\ntheorem Connected.pathNat_isContained [Infinite V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (hG : G.Connected) : pathNat ⊑ G := by\n  obtain ⟨v⟩ := hG.nonempty\n  -- u ≤ w iff u lies on a shortest path from v to w\n  let _ : PartialOrder V :=\n    { le u w := G.dist v u + G.dist u w = G.dist v w\n      le_refl u := by simp\n      le_trans u w x h₁ h₂ := by\n        have := hG.dist_triangle (u := v) (v := u) (w := x)\n        have := hG.dist_triangle (u := u) (v := w) (w := x)\n        grind\n      le_antisymm u w h₁ h₂ := by\n        have := G.dist_comm (u := u) (v := w)\n        exact hG.dist_eq_zero_iff.1 (by grind) }\n  -- G.dist v is strictly monotone, so the order is well founded\n  have hdist : StrictMono (G.dist v) := fun u w h => by\n    have := hG.pos_dist_of_ne h.ne\n    have : G.dist v u + G.dist u w = G.dist v w := h.le\n    grind\n  have : WellFoundedLT V := hdist.wellFoundedLT\n  -- if w covers u then they are adjacent\n  have hadj {u w : V} (h : u ⋖ w) : G.Adj u w := by\n    obtain ⟨_ | @⟨_, x, _, hux, p⟩, hp⟩ := hG.exists_walk_length_eq_dist u w\n    · exact absurd rfl h.ne\n    rw [Walk.length_cons] at hp\n    have := dist_le p\n    have := dist_eq_one_iff_adj.2 hux\n    have := hG.dist_triangle (u := v) (v := u) (w := x)\n    have := hG.dist_triangle (u := v) (v := x) (w := w)\n    have : G.dist v u + G.dist u w = G.dist v w := h.le\n    obtain rfl | rfl := h.eq_or_eq (c := x)\n      (show G.dist v u + G.dist u x = G.dist v x by grind)\n      (show G.dist v x + G.dist x w = G.dist v w by grind)\n    · exact (hux.ne rfl).elim\n    · exact hux\n  -- so each u has finitely many covers, and every vertex lies above v\n  obtain ⟨f, -, hf⟩ := exists_orderEmbedding_covby_of_forall_covby_finite\n    (fun u => (G.neighborSet u).toFinite.subset fun _ h => hadj h)\n    (Set.infinite_univ.mono fun w _ => (by simp : G.dist v v + G.dist v w = _))\n  refine ⟨⟨⟨f, ?_⟩, f.injective⟩⟩\n  rintro i j (rfl | rfl)\n  · exact hadj (hf i)\n  · exact (hadj (hf j)).symm\n\n\nLike the De Bruijn–Erdős theorem (colorable_of_forall_finite_subgraph_colorable)\nand Hall's theorem for infinite graphs (exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le),\nthis is a compactness argument, deducing an infinite object from finite\ninformation.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Infinite Graphs*","header":"13.5.1. Kőnig's Lemma","id":"/Theorems/#section-k-nig-s-lemma"}});